    Sets the track play count. For podcasts also maintains the iPod's
    'unplayed' bullet: -p 0 marks the episode unplayed (bullet shown),
    any count above zero marks it played.

 -- Ilya Kargapolov <d3vil.st@gmail.com>  Fri, 03 Jul 2026 00:00:00 +0200

gpod-utils (1.5.2) UNRELEASED; urgency=medium

  * Re-evaluate smart playlists on gpod-cp/gpod-rm library changes

    New gpod_spl_refresh() in the shared lib re-runs itdb_spl_update() on
    every smart playlist and reports how many changed membership. Called
    before the final DB write when tracks were added (gpod-cp) or removed
    (gpod-rm), so limited playlists refill and rule matches (eg 'added <
    1d') pick up new tracks without a manual 'gpod-playlist -U'.

  * Fix use-after-free in gpod-cp when a podcast copy fails

    Tracks are added to the master playlist - and, for -m podcast, the
    Podcasts playlist - before the file copy is attempted. On copy failure
    only the master playlist reference was removed; itdb_track_remove()
    frees the track without cleaning playlist refs, leaving the Podcasts
    playlist holding a dangling pointer that the subsequent forced
    itdb_write() traverses. Failed tracks are now removed from all
    playlists before the free.

 -- Ilya Kargapolov <d3vil.st@gmail.com>  Fri, 03 Jul 2026 00:00:00 +0200

gpod-utils (1.5.1) UNRELEASED; urgency=medium

  * Add smart playlist support to gpod-playlist

    Create smart playlists with '-c <name> -S', giving rules as repeatable
    -e '<field> <op> <value>' args (matching ALL rules by default, -A for
    ANY) and an optional limit -L <n>:<type>[:<sort>], eg 50:songs:recent.

    Rules cover string fields (= != ~ !~ ^ $), int fields including ranges
    (= a..b) in user-facing units (rating in stars, time in seconds; >=/<=
    translated exactly to libgpod's >/<), booleans (= set/unset) and dates
    as 'in the last' (added < 6m; units h/d/w/m/y).

    Listing a smart playlist (-l -p <name>) prints match mode, liveupdate,
    limit and rules in the same syntax the parser accepts; -l shows rule
    count and liveupdate per smart playlist.

    New -U operation re-evaluates all smart playlists against the library.
    The iPod does not evaluate rules itself - it displays the member list
    stored in iTunesDB - so members are populated at creation
    (itdb_spl_update) and -U refreshes them after gpod-cp/gpod-rm changes.

 -- Ilya Kargapolov <d3vil.st@gmail.com>  Thu, 02 Jul 2026 00:00:00 +0200

gpod-utils (1.5.0) UNRELEASED; urgency=medium

  * Add gpod-playlist: playlist CRUD tool for iPod/iTunesDB

    New binary supporting list (-l), create (-c), delete (-d), rename (-R),
    clear (-C) and per-track add/remove (-a/-r) operations on playlists.
    Tracks are addressed by iTunesDB id or ipod_path as reported by gpod-ls.

    Safety guards: master playlist cannot be deleted, cleared or have tracks
    added/removed; podcasts playlist cannot be deleted, renamed or have
    tracks added (use gpod-cp -m podcast); smart playlist contents are
    read-only. Renaming the master playlist is allowed and renames the iPod
    as displayed in iTunes (device name is stored as the mpl name).

  * Remove gpod-recent-pl; functionality merged into gpod-playlist -u

    'gpod-playlist -u [-n limit] [-3]' generates the same 'recently added'
    playlists (0wk/1wk/1month/3months/6months/12months) via the existing
    gpod_playlist_recent(), with -n album limit and optional m3u export.
    The gpod dispatcher command list and docs updated accordingly.

 -- Ilya Kargapolov <d3vil.st@gmail.com>  Thu, 02 Jul 2026 00:00:00 +0200

gpod-utils (1.4.21) UNRELEASED; urgency=medium

  * Fix FLAC-to-ALAC transcode producing wrong sample rate, duration and pitch

    gpod_ff_transcode_ctx_init hardcoded samplerate=48000 for the ALAC encoder,
    forcing an unnecessary 44100→48000 Hz upsample on every FLAC source. Combined
    with a second bug in load_convert_and_store — swr_convert was called with
    output_codec_context->frame_size (4096) as the output cap instead of the
    correctly rescaled sample count — the SWR resampler accumulated ~364 samples
    of delay per block, never flushed, resulting in ~21 seconds of audio missing
    from the end of a 4-minute track. Because the output contained the same
    sample count as the 44100 Hz source but was labelled 48000 Hz, the iPod
    played it back at 91.9% speed with raised pitch and truncated duration.

    Fix 1: remove hardcoded samplerate=48000 from the ALAC encoder init; rate
    now inherits from the input file.

    Fix 2: _select_samplerate now caps at GPOD_MAX_SAMPLERATE (48000) for codecs
    that report no supported-rates list (ALAC accepts any rate), preserving the
    input rate for ≤48000 Hz sources and downsampling only when strictly needed.

    Fix 3: load_convert_and_store passes the av_rescale_rnd-computed max_out_samples
    to swr_convert instead of frame_size, draining the resampler delay buffer
    correctly on every block.

  * Fix out-of-spec ALAC files copied verbatim to iPod instead of transcoded

    gpod_ff_scan marked all ALAC as supported_ipod_fmt=true regardless of sample
    rate or channel count, so a 96kHz or surround ALAC was block-copied to the
    iPod, which cannot play it. Now gated on the same limits as the transcode
    path: rate ≤ GPOD_MAX_SAMPLERATE (48000 Hz) and channels ≤ 2. Out-of-spec
    files fall through to gpod_ff_transcode where _select_samplerate downsamples
    and the channel cap downmixes to stereo.

    Move GPOD_MAX_SAMPLERATE define from gpod-ffmpeg-transcode.c into the shared
    gpod-ffmpeg.h so both translation units reference the same constant.

  * Make ALAC channel count and sample format conditional on input

    Previously channels=2 was forced unconditionally (upmixing mono to stereo)
    and sample_fmt=s16p was hardcoded (degrading 24-bit FLAC from s32p to s16p).

    Channels are now capped at the configured maximum (2 for iPod) but not
    upmixed: mono stays mono, stereo stays stereo, surround is downmixed.

    Sample format is auto-detected in open_output_file: the planar equivalent of
    the input format is used if the encoder supports it (s16→s16p, s32→s32p),
    preserving bit-depth for high-resolution sources. Falls back to the encoder's
    default when the planar variant is unsupported. Uses avcodec_get_supported_config
    on FFmpeg ≥7.0 (libavcodec ≥61) instead of the deprecated sample_fmts field.

 -- Ilya Kargapolov <d3vil.st@gmail.com>  Mon, 30 Jun 2026 00:00:00 +0200

gpod-utils (1.4.20) UNRELEASED; urgency=medium

  * Fix albumartist, sort tags and compilation flag not synced to iPod

    gpod_ff_meta_to_track was silently dropping three fields despite correct
    parsing: albumartist (album_artist), compilation flag, and all sort fields
    (sort_artist, sort_title, sort_album, sort_albumartist, sort_composer).

    Sort fields now prefer the file's own sort tag with gpod_sortname ("The "
    strip) as fallback. M4A files expose sort atoms under different ffmpeg
    dictionary keys than MP3: soar->"sort_artist", soal->"sort_album",
    sonm->"sort_name", soaa->"sort_album_artist", soco->"sort_composer"; add
    these to md_map_generic. MP3 was already covered via "artist-sort" and
    "album-sort" in md_map_generic and TSO2/TSOC in md_map_id3. The compilation
    key normalises to "compilation" for all formats (MP3 TCMP, M4A cpil, FLAC).

  * Expose compilation flag in gpod-ls output

    Add compilation to JSON output (brief and verbose modes) and SQLite export;
    the schema column already existed.

 -- Ilya Kargapolov <d3vil.st@gmail.com>  Sun, 29 Jun 2026 00:00:00 +0200

gpod-utils (1.4.19) UNRELEASED; urgency=medium

  * Improve cover art handling in extract_coverart

    Use device-specific resolution caps derived from libgpod's artwork
    capability tables: 320x320 for iPod Classic (6/6.5/7g, per
    ipod_classic_1_cover_art_info), 200x200 for iPod Video 1/2 (per
    ipod_video_cover_art_info), 300x300 for all other devices.

    Only downscale when the source exceeds the target: if the source is
    already within the cap and is JPEG, attach raw bytes directly to avoid
    a redundant decode/re-encode cycle; if within cap but non-JPEG, convert
    to JPEG at original dimensions without upscaling.

    Switch sws scaling algorithm from SWS_BILINEAR to SWS_AREA (pixel area
    averaging, correct for downscaling). Fix encode_frame_to_jpeg quality:
    add AV_CODEC_FLAG_QSCALE so the MJPEG encoder honours global_quality,
    and set q=2*FF_QP2LAMBDA (near-lossless); the previous formula was
    broken by operator precedence and the flag was missing entirely, leaving
    the encoder at its internal default.

 -- Ilya Kargapolov <d3vil.st@gmail.com>  Sun, 29 Jun 2026 00:00:00 +0200

